%% \part{The main stuff}


\section{What is this about}
\label{sec:what-this-about}


A document to clarify the relationship between confidence intervals and p-values, using first a one-sample t-test and then examples from the two-sample t-test.


\section{One-sample t}

Here we show, with an example, the ``This is a 95\% confidence interval for the true mean; that is, the set of (hypothetical) mean values from which the data do not deviate significantly. It is based on inverting the t test by solving for the values of \(\mu_0\) [\(\mu_0\) is the hypothesized true mean] that cause t to lie within its acceptance region.''.



<<>>=
set.seed(1)
data1 <- rnorm(20, mean = 2, sd = 1)
mean(data1)

t.test(data1)

## A value of the null just barely outside the CI. Notice the confidence
## interval does not change
t.test(data1, mu = 1.76)

## A value of the null at the boundary of the CI (within rounding error)
t.test(data1, mu = 1.76311)

## How is this CI computed, using the numbers from the t.test itself?
ts1 <- t.test(data1)

## Lower limit: t_{0.975, 19}: the 0.975 quantile of a t distr. with 19 df
## mean - s * t_{0.975, 19}
mean(data1) - ts1$stderr * qt(0.975, 19)
## Upper limit: same thing, but with a +
mean(data1) + ts1$stderr * qt(0.975, 19)

## Of course
all.equal(ts1$stderr, sqrt(var(data1)/length(data1)))

## What values of mu are covered by the CI?
##   all mu such that their computed t statistic
##   lies inside the rejection region

## What is the rejection region?
c(qt(0.025, 19), qt(0.975, 19))
## or, equivalently
c(-qt(0.975, 19),  qt(0.975, 19))

## How do we compute the t?
my_t <- function(candidate_mu,
                 sample_mean = mean(data1),
                 stderr = sqrt(var(data1)/length(data1))) {
  return((sample_mean -candidate_mu)/stderr)
}

are_you_inside_the_CI <- function(candidate_mu,
                                  cover = 0.95,
                                  sample_mean = mean(data1),
                                  stderr = sqrt(var(data1)/length(data1)),
                                  n = length(data1)) {
  CI_t_limits <- c(-qt(1 - (1 - cover)/2, df = (n - 1)),  qt(1 - (1 - cover)/2, df = (n - 1)))
  the_computed_t <- my_t(candidate_mu, sample_mean, stderr)
  if ((CI_t_limits[1] < the_computed_t) && (the_computed_t < CI_t_limits[2])) {
    return(TRUE)
  } else {
    return(FALSE)
  }
}

## If mu were 1.6
my_t(1.6)
are_you_inside_the_CI(1.6)
## A few others
are_you_inside_the_CI(4)
are_you_inside_the_CI(2)

## Many candidates
candidates <- seq(from = 1, to = 3, length.out = 50000)
inside_the_CI <- sapply(candidates, are_you_inside_the_CI)
first_inside <- which(inside_the_CI)[1]
last_inside <- which(inside_the_CI)
last_inside <- last_inside[length(last_inside)]
limits <- candidates[c(first_inside, last_inside)]


plot(x = c(1, 3), y = c(1, 2), axes = FALSE, type = "n", xlab = "", ylab = "")
segments(x0 = 1, y0 = 1, x1 = limits[1], y1 = 1, col = "salmon", lwd = 2)
segments(x0 = 3, y0 = 1, x1 = limits[2], y1 = 1, col = "salmon", lwd = 2)
segments(x0 = limits[1], y0 = 1, x1 = limits[2], y1 = 1, col = "black", lwd = 2)
axis(1, at = limits, labels = limits)

set.seed(NULL) ## reset seed
@

Now: how would the CI be larger or smaller if we asked for a 99\% or a 90\% CI?


\section{CI and p-value relationships in the t-test}
First, we read the data as we did in the main notes:

<<>>=
dp53 <- read.table("P53.txt", header = TRUE,
                   stringsAsFactors = TRUE)
@


Now, we do the same two-sample t-test we did there. Because I will reuse some output, I will store the object.

<<firstt>>=
(tt1 <- t.test(p53 ~ cond, data = dp53))
@

Before doing some numerical explorations, let us plot the t-distribution. You can do this in several different ways from R. I will use function \texttt{plotDistr} from package \texttt{RcmdrMisc}. I will draw a t distribution with 17.402 degrees of freedom, the same degrees of freedom as in our test above\footnote{It might have been simpler to use the ``non-Welch'' test, the one that assumes variances are the same, so as to not to have to use non-integer degrees of freedom. But our original example uses Welch, so we will stick to that.}.



<<qs,echo=FALSE, results='hide'>>=
q10 <- qt(p = 0.050, df = 17.402)
q05 <- qt(p = 0.025, df = 17.402)
q001 <- qt(p = 0.0005, df = 17.402)
@


<<tdist>>=
library(RcmdrMisc)
.x <- seq(-4.1, 4.1, length.out=1000)

plotDistr(.x, dt(.x, df=17.402), cdf=FALSE, xlab="x",
          ylab = "Density",
          main = paste(c("t Distribution.",
                         "Degrees of freedom = 17.402")))
abline(v = -2.34019, lty = 2)
abline(v = 2.34019, lty = 2)

abline(v = q10, lty = 5, col = "orange")
abline(v = -q10, lty = 5, col = "orange")


abline(v = q05, lty = 3, col = "blue")
abline(v = -q05, lty = 3, col = "blue")

abline(v = q001, lty = 4, col = "red")
abline(v = -q001, lty = 4, col = "red")

legend(x = -1, y = 0.15,
       legend = c(
         "p = 0.0314; t = 2.34",
         paste0("p = 0.10; t = ", round(-q10, 2)),
         paste0("p = 0.05; t = ", round(-q05, 2)),
         paste0("p = 0.001; t = ", round(-q001, 2))
       ),
       lty = c(2, 5, 3, 4),
       col = c("black", "orange", "blue", "red"))

rm(.x)
@

The figure shows lines that delimit four regions:

\begin{itemize}

\item \textbf{0.10}: the orange lines denote rejection regions for tests at the \(\alpha = 0.10\): the area to right of the right orange line + the area to the left of the left orange line adds to 0.10. So any value of the t-statistic larger than 1.74 or smaller than -1.74 will lead to rejection at \(\alpha = 0.10\).  A test with a test statistic of exactly 1.74 will have a p-value of exactly 0.10.


\item \textbf{0.05}: the blue lines denote rejection regions for tests at the \(\alpha = 0.05\): the area to right of the right blue line + the area to the left of the left blue line adds to 0.05. So any value of the t-statistic larger than 2.11 or smaller than -2.11 will lead to rejection at \(\alpha = 0.05\).  A test with a test statistic of exactly 2.11 will have a p-value of exactly 0.05.


\item \textbf{0.0314}. This is ``our test''.  The black lines denote rejection regions for tests at the \(\alpha = 0.0314\): the area to right of the right black line + the area to the left of the left black line add to 0.0314. So any value of the t-statistic larger than 2.34 or smaller than -2.34 (hey! our test statistic) will lead to rejection at \(\alpha = 0.0314\).  A test with a test statistic of exactly -2.34 will have a p-value of exactly 0.0314; just like our test.


\item \textbf{0.001}. The red line. At test with a test statistic of exactly 3.95 will have a p-value of 0.001.

\end{itemize}

Now, I am going to get confidence intervals. I am going to use a  brute-force approach: I am going to run t-tests with our data, and modify the argument that asks for the size of the confidence interval. % (yes, you can modify this via R Commander)
There are other ways to get confidence intervals, but I want to use output/methods that are familiar to us.


First, I will use a confidence region of exactly 1 - p.value, the p-value we actually get from the t-test. (I specify the confidence level by passing the value of the p.value, instead of typing it).

<<out.width = '\\textwidth'>>=
t.test(p53 ~ cond, data = dp53, conf.level = 1 - tt1$p.value)
@
Notice that right limit of the confidence interval is basically 0. (R prints the header as ``97 percent confidence interval'': the level is 1 - 0.0314 = 0.9686, which R rounds to 97.)


Now, obtain another three confidence intervals, of 90\%, 95\%, and 99.9\%:


<<>>=
t.test(p53 ~ cond, data = dp53, conf.level = 0.9)
@


<<>>=
t.test(p53 ~ cond, data = dp53, conf.level = 0.95)
@


<<>>=
t.test(p53 ~ cond, data = dp53, conf.level = 0.999)
@
If the header says ``100 percent confidence interval'': that is 99.9 rounded by R when printing. A true 100\% confidence interval would be the whole real line.


Now, please take a piece of paper and draw a figure like this:

